bac2food — licensing overview
=============================

This project ships two different kinds of thing under two different licences.
Which one applies to you depends on what you are using.

  SOFTWARE  ->  GNU Affero General Public License v3.0     (see LICENSE-CODE.md)
                Free for academic and research use. A separate commercial
                licence is available from the authors.

  DATA      ->  CC BY 4.0 for most of it, with two partitions that keep the
                terms they inherited from their source (see LICENSE-DATA.md)

Why the data cannot carry one blanket licence
---------------------------------------------

The harmonized composition table is assembled from 15 independently licensed
food-composition databases. Two of those licences are copyleft and they are not
compatible with each other, let alone with a single project-wide term:

  * AFCD (Australia) is CC BY-SA 3.0 AU. ShareAlike means a derivative must
    stay ShareAlike, which permits commercial use. It CANNOT be relicensed
    as NonCommercial.
  * The three FAO/INFOODS tables are CC BY-NC-SA 3.0 IGO. NonCommercial means
    a derivative must stay NonCommercial. It CANNOT be relicensed as
    permissive, and it cannot be sold.

There is no single licence that satisfies both. Anyone proposing to put the
whole table under one term — including a NonCommercial one — should read those
two lines again first. The release is therefore partitioned by source, and
`licences.tsv` in the deposit says which rows carry which terms, joined to the
composition table on `source_db`. Filtering to a licence you can live with is
one join.

The per-source terms, versions and required attribution strings are in
`licences.tsv` and in Supplementary Table S1 of the accompanying Data
Descriptor. Each was confirmed against the provider's own primary terms.
